        },
        {
          "t": "Chromecast as an alternative, if going that route",
          "done": false
        },
        {
          "t": "Table stand or mount so the TV sits at eye level",
          "done": false
        },
        {
          "t": "AOKCOS floor TV stand w/ shelf — ~$83 Amazon.ca",
          "done": false
        },
        {
          "t": "Confirm the TV's VESA pattern — done: 200mm centres, standard, fits",
          "done": true
        },
        {
          "t": "Weight or position the glass base — dogs in a small booth",
          "done": false
        }
      ],
      "art": [],
      "notes": "Rob's small TV, laptop as backup. Test the whole chain end to end before the event, and copy the loop locally so it plays without internet. Decide HDMI vs Chromecast on reliability. OPTION 12 Aug: AOKCOS floor TV stand, ~$83 on Amazon.ca (black; white $78.84). Fits 32-70in, max VESA 400x400, holds 88lbs, 8-level height adjustment, swivel + 12deg tilt, tempered glass base, integrated shelf. 4.5/5 from 48 ratings. This is better than putting the TV on the table: it frees the whole back row (~34in of table width), puts the screen at standing eye height instead of 38in, the shelf holds the laptop feeding it, and the swivel means we can reorient on the day once the floor plan is final. It is also reusable kit Paw Prints keeps, not a consumable. CONFIRMED WITH LEANNE (23 Jul): TV over laptop-only — \"much easier for people walking by to see\", HDMI or Chromecast, laptop as backup. NOT yet raised with her: the floor stand, the $83, a backup TV, or whether she would prefer her own set.",
      "questions": [
        {
          "q": "Floor stand vs TV on the table? Stand is ~$83, frees ~34in of table width, gets the screen to standing eye height and holds the laptop on its shelf. Downside is ~18in of floor footprint in a tight booth.",
          "a": "Rob, 13 Aug: going with the floor stand. Consumer stands sit too low for standing viewers — screen centre lands ~45-50in where you want 55-60in — so he searched the \"mobile TV cart\" category instead and settled on the AOKCOS at ~$83 as the best fit for the price range.",
          "who": "Rob"
        },
        {
          "q": "What is the VESA mount pattern on the TV we are bringing?",
          "a": "Rob, 12 Aug: the lend TV HAS proper VESA bosses — threaded zinc inserts confirmed by photo, with case screws alongside them (which is what made the pattern hard to read at first). Still to measure: centre-to-centre spacing in mm, and the bolt thread (M4/M6/M8). Also still to confirm the set has HDMI — a VGA port on the connector panel dates it to roughly 2010-2015. MEASURED 13 Aug: 200mm between hole centres = a standard VESA 200 pattern (200x200 or 200x100 — vertical still to confirm, but it cannot be 400 since the panel is only ~398mm tall). Well within the stand's 400x400 max, and a mount rated for 32-70in screens must support small patterns by definition. Remaining checks: the listing's stated MINIMUM VESA, and that the hardware pack covers M6/M8 with spacers — the bosses looked recessed.",
          "who": "Rob"
        },
        {
          "q": "Do you like the floor-stand idea for the TV? ~$83, puts the screen at standing eye height instead of 38in on a table, frees about 34in of table width, and its shelf holds the laptop that feeds it. It is reusable kit you keep rather than a consumable.",
          "a": "",
          "who": "Leanne"
        },
        {
          "q": "Do you have a backup TV, in case the one we are lending turns out not to fit the stand? Or would you rather use one of your own regardless?",
          "a": "",
          "who": "Leanne"
        }
      ],
      "cost": 83
    },
